When a caret step crosses from one text node to the next, Ladybird now
takes the two boundaries for one position only if nothing between them
renders text, is non-editable (even when empty), or is a replaced or
atomic inline box. Blink does the same: MostBackwardCaretPosition()
and MostForwardCaretPosition() in visible_units.cc never cross a change
of editability, and they stop at any node whose ends are visually
distinct, by EndsOfNodeAreVisuallyDistinctPositions(). WebKit does the
same in Position::upstream() and Position::downstream(), with its own
endsOfNodeAreVisuallyDistinctPositions().

Run in Chrome 158, Firefox 151, and WebKit 26.5, Shift+Right from just
in front of a non-editable “★” selects just the “★” in all three, as in
Ladybird now. With an empty non-editable span there instead, one step
lands at its far side with nothing selected, in all three: Firefox and
WebKit put it at the start of the next text node, as Ladybird now does,
and Chrome at the parent boundary after the span.
